Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] shared-blocker check lane: kill surviving mutants
 M06/M15/M19 (+C10)

The post-merge audit of PR #2923 (t_01a1d862ef64b986) found three mutants
that passed all 78 tests in tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_shared_blocker_*.py.
This adds behavioural tests for each, plus the earlier C10 survivor.
No production code changes.

New tests (tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_shared_blocker_check_edges.py):
- M06 (`if ended == newest[bid]:` -> `if True:`): SQLite sorts a TEXT
  ended_at after every INTEGER one. So an older legacy ISO-stamped assertion
  (which _to_epoch supports) arrives last in the ORDER BY ended_at scan. The
  test checks that it does not join the newest cohort, which would change the
  token and revoke a verified check success. Under sorted integer data the
  guard would be equivalent; the text/integer ordering split is what makes it
  live.
- M15 (`set(st.behind) - foreign` dropped): two cards are behind the wall and
  the foreign one sorts first. The evidence must land on the home card and
  clear the wall. Under the mutant, the in-txn re-check skips the foreign
  homes[0], so nothing is recorded.
- C10 (in-txn `homes[0] in foreign_ids(conn)` re-check dropped): the home card
  turns foreign while the shell check runs, so no check or attempt event is
  written.
- M19 (fence `or checked > now:` -> `or False:`): a rc=0 receipt for an older
  cohort, stamped after the evaluation clock (skewed remote or replay), must
  not fence a newer assertion. Once the clock reaches the receipt it counts as
  normal historical recovery again.

Proof (isolated HOME, sdlc-review scripts/mutants.py, all 11
test_kanban_shared_blocker_*.py files):
